    {
      "id": "p0034-b002",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Sometimes, the lack of external information can cause a literal translation to fail later on. This is common when translating ongoing entertainment series. In these situations, it's possible that only the original creator knows where the story will go. Or it's possible that the creator has no idea and is making things up as they go. Either way, without input from the creator, a translator can only hope and guess which insignificant things will become significant later on.",
        "raw": "Sometimes, the lack of external information can cause a literal translation to\nfail later on. This is common when translating ongoing entertainment series.\nIn these situations, it's possible that only the original creator knows where the\nstory will go. Or it's possible that the creator has no idea and is making things\nup as they go. Either way, without input from the creator, a translator can only\nhope and guess which insignificant things will become significant later on.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29266753,
          0.09147857,
          0.88850039,
          0.19522857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.28929936,
          0.09076429,
          0.90150918,
          0.1963
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          2,
          3,
          4,
          5,
          6,
          7
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "有时缺乏外部信息，会让当时的直译在日后出错。翻译仍在连载的娱乐作品时，这很常见：可能只有原作者知道故事将走向哪里，也可能作者自己并不知道，正边写边想。无论哪种情况，没有作者提供信息，译者只能猜测哪些看似无关紧要的细节会在以后变得重要，并希望自己猜中。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
      "annotations": [],
      "layout": {
        "bbox": [
